          reading: {
            title: 'Reading', titleUk: 'Читання', emoji: '🏠',
            textTitle: "Anya\u2019s grandma\u2019s house \u2014 a tour through time",
            text: [
              "Every Sunday, I visit my grandma\u2019s house. It\u2019s a small wooden house on a quiet street in Lviv \u2014 the kind of house that has been standing there for over 100 years. And inside every room, there\u2019s a story told in materials.",
              "Room 1 \u2014 the entrance.",
              "Right now, as I walk in, grandma is polishing an ancient wooden staircase. It creaks under her hand. She has been polishing it every Sunday for 40 years. \u201cGood wood needs love,\u201d she says. On the wall hangs her father\u2019s old leather coat \u2014 heavy, dark, worn on the sleeves. He wore it in 1943 during the war. It still smells faintly of pipe tobacco.",
              "Room 2 \u2014 the kitchen.",
              "In the kitchen, grandma keeps a set of silver spoons in a wooden box. They were her mother\u2019s. When I arrived last Sunday, she was polishing them one by one. \u201cSilver blackens if you ignore it,\u201d she said, \u201cbut it comes back beautifully.\u201d She was wearing her old cotton apron with tiny embroidered flowers. \u201cThis apron is older than your mother,\u201d she smiled.",
              "Room 3 \u2014 the living room.",
              "The living room is the material museum. There\u2019s a heavy oak table from 1920. A shiny brass lamp that was made in Vienna. A soft wool blanket in the classic Ukrainian red-and-black pattern. And a transparent glass cabinet full of tiny gold objects: a ring my great-grandma wore on her wedding day, a small cross my grandpa was given at his baptism, a coin from 1898.",
              "Right now, grandma is sitting on the sofa, reading a book about traditional Ukrainian weaving. She looks up. \u201cAnya, do you know what wool taught me?\u201d",
              "\u201cNo?\u201d",
              "\u201cThat the softest things last the longest.\u201d",
              "Room 4 \u2014 the bedroom.",
              "Grandma\u2019s bed is covered with a wool blanket her mother made in 1958. Above the bed hangs a small silver icon of Saint Sophia. On the bedside table sits a heavy metal clock that has been ticking for exactly 63 years. It was a wedding gift.",
              "\u201cThis clock will still be ticking long after I\u2019m gone,\u201d grandma says. \u201cMetal is patient like that.\u201d",
              "Room 5 \u2014 the attic.",
              "The attic is my favourite. It\u2019s full of things nobody uses anymore but nobody can throw away. Old cotton dresses from the 1960s. A pair of leather shoes that grandma wore to her first job interview. A shiny silver mirror with a tiny crack. Yellowing paper letters tied with a red string.",
              "Last week I was searching through a wooden trunk when I found something amazing: my grandma\u2019s childhood notebook, from 1955, when she was 12. Same age as me. She had drawn a picture of herself wearing a wool sweater and standing next to a golden autumn tree.",
              "Under the drawing, she had written: \u201cWhen I grow up, I will be sitting in a warm house full of beautiful things. And I will still be wearing wool.\u201d",
              "She was right.",
              "The future \u2014 or, my future visits.",
              "This time next year, I will still be walking up her wooden staircase every Sunday. Grandma will probably still be polishing something \u2014 silver, wood, glass, anything that shines back at her. I\u2019ll be sitting in the kitchen drinking tea from a transparent glass, wearing whatever sweater she\u2019s knitted me most recently.",
              "This time in 10 years, I might be visiting a different house, in a different city, with different materials. But wherever I go, some things will follow me. A silver spoon from her set. A wool blanket. The paper letters tied in red string.",
              "Because that\u2019s what grandmothers do. They pass on the materials. And the materials carry the stories.",
              "That\u2019s the whole lesson."
            ],
            questions: [
              { q: 'How long has grandma been polishing her wooden staircase every Sunday?', options: ['5 years', '40 years', 'Just a few months'], correct: 1 },
              { q: 'What is the leather coat in the entrance from?', options: ['A shop last year', 'Her father, from the war in 1943', 'Nick\u2019s dad'], correct: 1 },
              { q: 'What did Anya\u2019s grandma say silver does if you ignore it?', options: ['Nothing at all', 'It blackens \u2014 but comes back beautifully with polishing', 'It breaks'], correct: 1 },
              { q: 'What lesson did wool teach grandma?', options: ['That the softest things last the longest', 'That wool is very expensive', 'That wool is itchy'], correct: 0 },
              { q: 'How old is the metal clock in the bedroom?', options: ['5 years', 'Exactly 63 years \u2014 a wedding gift', 'Just 10 years'], correct: 1 },
              { q: 'What did grandma write in her childhood notebook from 1955?', options: ['\u201cI want to be a queen.\u201d', '\u201cWhen I grow up, I will be sitting in a warm house full of beautiful things. And I will still be wearing wool.\u201d', '\u201cI hate wool.\u201d'], correct: 1 },
              { q: 'What does Anya predict she will be doing this time NEXT YEAR?', options: ['Moving away to a big city', 'Still walking up grandma\u2019s wooden staircase every Sunday', 'Never visiting again'], correct: 1 },
              { q: 'What is the whole lesson of the story?', options: ['Grandmothers pass on the materials. And the materials carry the stories.', 'Old things should be thrown away.', 'Only gold matters.'], correct: 0 }
            ]
          },

