          vocabPresentation: {
            title: 'Vocabulary Presentation', titleUk: 'Презентація словника',
            intro: "Welcome to one of the most honest lessons of the year. Today you\u2019ll get 8 words for different TYPES of friends in your life \u2014 from the closest (best friend, close friend, old friend) to the furthest (acquaintance, stranger). Plus 4 essential phrases about what happens BETWEEN friends over time \u2014 keeping in touch, losing touch, falling out, and finding people with whom you have a lot in common. English has precise words for every type of relationship. Learning them helps you understand YOUR OWN life more clearly.",
            introUk: 'Ласкаво просимо на один з найчесніших уроків року. Сьогодні — 8 слів для різних ТИПІВ друзів у твоєму житті — від найближчих (best friend, close friend, old friend) до найвіддаленіших (acquaintance, stranger). Плюс 4 обовʼязкові фрази про те, що відбувається МІЖ друзями з часом — тримати звʼязок, губити, посваритися, знайти людей з якими є багато спільного. Англійська має точні слова для кожного типу стосунків. Вивчати їх — значить чіткіше розуміти ВЛАСНЕ життя.',
            items: [
              // Types of friends - closest first
              { emoji: '💛', en: 'best friend', uk: 'найкращий(-а) друг(-а)', explain: "The #1 friend. The one you tell everything to. Usually one person, sometimes two. \u201cSofia is my best friend since we were 5.\u201d The classic.", accent: 'sage' },
              { emoji: '💚', en: 'close friend', uk: 'близький(-а) друг(-а)', explain: "Very good friend, but not necessarily #1. You can have several close friends. \u201cMaya and Nick are close friends of mine.\u201d More flexible than \u201cbest friend\u201d.", accent: 'sage' },
              { emoji: '🕰️', en: 'old friend', uk: 'давній(-я) друг(-а)', explain: "Someone you\u2019ve known for a LONG time, even if you don\u2019t see them often. \u201cWe\u2019ve been friends since kindergarten \u2014 we\u2019re old friends now.\u201d Time matters.", accent: 'mustard' },
              // Types of friends - contextual
              { emoji: '📚', en: 'classmate', uk: 'однокласник(-ця)', explain: "Someone in your class at school. May or may not be a friend! \u201cWe have 30 classmates but only 5 close friends among them.\u201d Context, not closeness.", accent: 'sky' },
              { emoji: '👥', en: 'groupmate', uk: 'одногрупник(-ця)', explain: "Someone in your group \u2014 a study group, sports team, band, or club. \u201cMy volleyball groupmates are amazing.\u201d Like classmates but not always school.", accent: 'sky' },
              { emoji: '👋', en: 'friend of a friend', uk: 'друг(-а) друга', explain: "Someone you know indirectly \u2014 through a friend. \u201cAndriy is a friend of a friend, we\u2019ve met a few times.\u201d Familiar but not close.", accent: 'plum' },
              { emoji: '🧊', en: 'acquaintance', uk: 'знайомий(-а)', explain: "Someone you know a little bit \u2014 name, face, hello \u2014 but you\u2019re not really friends. \u201cShe\u2019s an acquaintance from the gym.\u201d Polite but distant.", accent: 'plum' },
              { emoji: '🚶', en: 'stranger(s)', uk: 'незнайомець(-йомі)', explain: "Someone you don\u2019t know at all. \u201cDon\u2019t talk to strangers online.\u201d The opposite of every other word on this list.", accent: 'terracotta' },
              // Phrases about relationship dynamics
              { emoji: '📞', en: 'keep in touch', uk: 'тримати звʼязок', explain: "To stay connected \u2014 messages, calls, meetings \u2014 even when life gets busy. \u201cWe\u2019ve kept in touch since primary school.\u201d Requires effort. Worth it.", accent: 'sage' },
              { emoji: '🌫️', en: 'lose touch', uk: 'втратити звʼязок', explain: "The opposite of \u201ckeep in touch\u201d. To stop talking to someone without any fight \u2014 just life happens. \u201cWe lost touch after she moved to Kyiv.\u201d Sad but normal.", accent: 'plum' },
              { emoji: '🤝', en: 'have a lot in common', uk: 'мати багато спільного', explain: "To share many interests, opinions, or experiences. \u201cWe have a lot in common \u2014 same music, same books, same silly sense of humour.\u201d Foundation of friendship.", accent: 'mustard' },
              { emoji: '💔', en: 'fall out', uk: 'посваритися (серйозно)', explain: "To have a serious argument and stop being friends. \u201cThey fell out over a stupid rumour.\u201d Different from just \u201cargue\u201d \u2014 fall out means the friendship is BROKEN.", accent: 'terracotta' }
            ]
          },

